{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The decision problem does not contain a sure outcome (i.e., no outcome has 100% probability), so the reference value is zero. The reason evaluates each outcome by computing its difference from this reference value (i.e., the outcome itself, since reference = 0), then computes the mean (expected) difference, weighted by probabilities. For Lottery A: outcomes are -6000 (25%) and 0 (75%); differences from reference (0) are -6000 and 0, so mean difference = (0.25 × -6000) + (0.75 × 0) = -1500. For Lottery B: outcomes are -4000 (25%), -2000 (25%), and 0 (50%); differences are -4000, -2000, and 0, so mean difference = (0.25 × -4000) + (0.25 × -2000) + (0.5 × 0) = -1000 - 500 = -1500. Both lotteries have the same mean difference (-1500), so the reason would be indifferent between them.",
